# Settings for chatsbom. Copy this file to `.env` and fill in what you
# use; a copy left as it is changes nothing. `.env` is git-ignored and
# this file is not, so real values belong only in the copy.
#
# Three things read `.env`:
#   - the chatsbom CLI, from its working directory or the nearest parent
#     directory that has one;
#   - docker compose, from beside docker-compose.yaml, for the ${...}
#     settings in that file;
#   - the systemd units in deploy/systemd, through EnvironmentFile=.
# Kept at the root of a checkout, one file serves all three. For the CLI
# and for compose, a variable already set in the environment wins over
# the file.
#
# So it keeps to what all three parse alike: plain KEY=value lines,
# comments on lines of their own (systemd keeps a trailing "# ..." as
# part of the value), and no `export`.
#
# A commented-out setting shows its default, or an example where there
# is none. Uncomment one only to change it: set but empty is not unset,
# and the CLI would take the empty value over the default.


# --- Collection --------------------------------------------------------

# A GitHub token, for every `github` stage, `queue sync` and the compose
# collector. A fine-grained one with read access to public repositories
# is enough: https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens
GITHUB_TOKEN=
# How `github depgraph` and `run` fetch GitHub's dependency graph. Its
# synchronous endpoint closes on 2026-11-13, for a report that is
# generated and then fetched. `auto` asks the endpoint until then, a
# report when that fails, and only reports from that day; `async` asks
# for reports now, at a request or more extra per repository; `sync`
# asks the endpoint alone, and the stage turns itself off on that day;
# `off` turns the stage off now.
# CHATSBOM_DEPGRAPH_API=auto
# More GitHub tokens for the dependency-graph stage, beside GITHUB_TOKEN,
# comma-separated. GitHub meters that stage per token, so each one adds
# a worker paced to DEPGRAPH_RATE, running in parallel. Values are never
# logged; a token GitHub rejects is skipped. Example: ghp_aaa,ghp_bbb
# CHATSBOM_DEPGRAPH_TOKENS=
# More GitHub tokens for the collector that is to replace the pipeline
# (#155; nothing runs it yet), after GITHUB_TOKEN, comma-separated. Each
# serves every bucket, REST, GraphQL, search and the dependency graph,
# whichever has the most left. GitHub meters an account, so a token adds
# to the budget only when it is another account's. The tokens in
# CHATSBOM_DEPGRAPH_TOKENS move here when the collector replaces the
# pipeline. Values are never logged. Example: ghp_aaa,ghp_bbb
# CHATSBOM_GITHUB_TOKENS=
# What that collector leaves of each token's buckets for work run by hand
# with the same tokens, as bucket=count, comma-separated. A bucket named
# here is set, and core, graphql and search otherwise keep these.
# CHATSBOM_GITHUB_RESERVE=core=500,graphql=500,search=5


# --- docker compose ----------------------------------------------------

# Your uid, from `id -u`. The collector, lock and cli containers run as
# it: data/ and .cache/ are bind mounts owned by whoever cloned the repo,
# and as anyone else the ledger is a read-only database. Empty is 1000.
UID=
# Your gid, from `id -g`, for the same reason. Empty is 1000.
GID=


# --- ClickHouse, for the CLI on the host -------------------------------
# The compose services connect by service name, with settings of their
# own; the accounts are what they take from here: both for cli, the
# admin one for the collector, and the guest password for the dashboard.

# The server's host.
# CLICKHOUSE_HOST=localhost
# Its HTTP port.
# CLICKHOUSE_PORT=8123
# The database. Another name also needs its own GRANT for guest, in
# database/config/users.d/guest.xml.
# CLICKHOUSE_DB=chatsbom
# The account that indexes and exports: `db index`, `db edges`,
# `db raw` and `export`.
# CLICKHOUSE_ADMIN_USER=admin
# Its password. The accounts in database/config/users.d are development
# defaults; for a server reachable beyond localhost, change them there
# and set them here.
# CLICKHOUSE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin
# The read-only account that `db query`, `chat` and the dashboard use.
# CLICKHOUSE_GUEST_USER=guest
# Its password, which the compose dashboard and cli take from here too.
# CLICKHOUSE_GUEST_PASSWORD=guest


# --- chatsbom chat -----------------------------------------------------

# An Anthropic API key. The compose dashboard's AI answers use it too;
# without one, /api/chat says they are not configured.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Or an Anthropic auth token in its place, for `chat` alone.
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=
# Where `chat` sends its requests, and so the key or token above. Unset,
# that is Anthropic. Set it only for an Anthropic-compatible endpoint you
# mean to hand that token to — a gateway you run, or another provider's
# Messages API, used with that provider's token and never an Anthropic
# one.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://llm-gateway.example.com
# Show each cost converted too, at this many units of a currency to the
# US dollar. There is no default: a rate written down is soon wrong.
# CHATSBOM_COST_RATE=7.2
# The symbol of that currency.
# CHATSBOM_COST_SYMBOL=¥


# --- github classify ---------------------------------------------------

# A key for the OpenAI-compatible API that classifies repositories.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# That API's address, and so where the key goes. Unset, it is OpenAI's,
# https://api.openai.com/v1, asked for gpt-4o-mini; at any other, name
# one of its own models with --model. A local server needs no key at
# all, Ollama's for instance.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1


# --- chatsbom openapi --------------------------------------------------

# Where `openapi stats` keeps the tokenizer it downloads on its first
# run: 1.7 MB, from openaipublic.blob.core.windows.net. The setting is
# tiktoken's own, which chatsbom defaults.
# TIKTOKEN_CACHE_DIR=.cache/tiktoken


# --- Logging -----------------------------------------------------------

# How the CLI logs, on stderr: `console` for a person, or `json`, one
# object per line, for whatever collects the logs. The compose collector
# and depgraph services log JSON whatever this says.
# CHATSBOM_LOG_FORMAT=console
# The older switch, kept as an alias for deployments that set it:
# `production` means `json`. Where both are set, CHATSBOM_LOG_FORMAT wins.
# ENV=production


# --- The compose collector (docker compose --profile collect) ----------

# Seconds between revalidation slices.
# SYNC_INTERVAL_SECONDS=900
# Repositories a slice re-checks.
# SYNC_SLICE=500
# Rate-limited requests a slice may spend; a 304 costs none.
# SYNC_QUOTA=250
# Repositories the `chatsbom run` after each slice may advance through
# the stages that slice made due.
# RUN_LIMIT=50
# API requests that `run` may spend. The dependency graph is metered
# apart, and the `depgraph` service collects it, whatever this is.
# RUN_QUOTA=400
# Repositories each pass of the `depgraph` service fetches graphs for.
# DEPGRAPH_LIMIT=200
# Dependency-graph requests an hour, per token. GitHub allows 100 to 200.
# DEPGRAPH_RATE=90
# Seconds the `depgraph` service waits between passes.
# DEPGRAPH_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300
# Slices between index passes, `sbom generate`, `db raw --apply` and
# then `db index`: 96 of 15 minutes is about a day.
# INDEX_EVERY_SLICES=96
# Content roots the `sbom generate` of an index pass rescans at most, or
# all of them. The first pass after a Syft upgrade rescans every root,
# about seven hours for 28,000, and no slice runs meanwhile; a number of
# 1 or more spreads that over days, each pass taking up where the last
# stopped. 0 is refused, and fails that step of each index pass.
# GENERATE_LIMIT=all
# Slices between retention passes: 96 of 15 minutes is about a day.
# PRUNE_EVERY_SLICES=96
# Scans a retention pass keeps per repository, newest first.
# PRUNE_KEEP=2


# --- The compose dashboard (web) ---------------------------------------

# The address its port 8787 is published on, for a tunnel outside the
# project: every interface by default, the LAN included. 172.17.0.1 is
# the docker bridge alone on a default install; `ip -4 addr show docker0`
# says for certain. The tunnel mode, below, publishes no port to bind.
# WEB_BIND=0.0.0.0
# Turnstile's secret key, from a widget in the Cloudflare dashboard. Set,
# every question to the AI answers must first pass a Turnstile
# challenge: worth it on a public URL. It needs the site key below.
# TURNSTILE_SECRET=
# The same widget's site key, which the page shows the widget with. It
# is public, unlike the secret.
# TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# The site's hostnames, comma-separated, as `sbom.example.com`: a
# Turnstile token solved on any other page is refused. Unset, the host
# each request was sent to, which a client that reaches 8787 directly
# chooses; set it to the tunnel's.
# TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES=
# A secret a Cloudflare Transform Rule adds to every request for the
# site, as X-Edge-Secret. Set, the rate limiters believe CF-Connecting-IP
# only on a request carrying it, and one that reached 8787 directly
# shares a single bucket whatever address it claims (DEPLOY.md). The
# container's own checks carry it too, so that bucket cannot fail them.
# Not needed in the tunnel mode, where only the tunnel reaches 8787.
# EDGE_SECRET=
# The most the AI answers may spend in a UTC day, in US dollars. Each
# turn's worst case is held against it before the model is asked, so
# questions arriving together cannot take the day past it. 0 is no cap.
# `chatsbom web serve` reads it too, and does not start with a value
# that is not a number of dollars.
# DAILY_SPEND_CAP_USD=5
# Seconds between the container's checks that the Worker still answers.
# A wedged Worker does not exit, and the restart policy acts only on an
# exit, so the container exits itself after enough failed checks.
# WATCHDOG_INTERVAL_SECONDS=30
# Failed checks in a row that count as wedged.
# WATCHDOG_FAILURES=4
# Seconds one check waits for an answer.
# WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=20
# Seconds after the start before the first check.
# WATCHDOG_GRACE_SECONDS=60
# Any value turns the checks off, leaving a bare `wrangler dev`.
# WATCHDOG_DISABLED=


# --- The tunnel mode (docker-compose.tunnel.yaml) ----------------------

# The files compose reads. These two are the tunnel mode: `cloudflared`
# runs as a service beside the dashboard, whose port is then published
# nowhere (DEPLOY.md). Compose's own setting, taken from the `.env` in
# the directory it runs in, so run it from here. A
# docker-compose.override.yaml of your own goes last in the list: with
# this set, compose reads only the files it names.
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yaml:docker-compose.tunnel.yaml
# The tunnel's token, for a remotely managed tunnel, whose routes the
# Cloudflare dashboard holds: under Networking, Tunnels, the tunnel's
# install command ends in `--token` and this value. Without it the
# tunnel refuses to start, and says so in `docker compose logs`.
# TUNNEL_TOKEN=


# --- The web service (chatsbom web serve) ------------------------------
# Opt-in: on the host with the `web` extra, or as compose's `site`
# service (docker compose --profile site up -d), beside the dashboard
# above, which serves the site until the Python service replaces it
# (#128). It does not start with a setting it cannot read, and says
# which. Compose hands `site` each setting here as it is, but for
# WEB_STATE_DIR and WEB_SNAPSHOT, which name its mounts there.

# The key its proof-of-work challenges are signed with. It does not
# start without one of 32 characters or more: `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# ALTCHA_HMAC_KEY=
# The subnets, comma-separated, of the network only cloudflared is on:
# CF-Connecting-IP is believed from a peer there, and from no other.
# Compose gives its `edge` network this one and tells `site` it; set it
# to one IPv4 subnet to move both (DEPLOY.md). Unset on the host, the
# header is believed from no one.
# EDGE_SUBNET=172.16.128.0/24
# Where it keeps web.sqlite: the day's spend, and the challenges used.
# WEB_STATE_DIR=data
# At most LIMIT challenges, and questions, from one client in any
# PERIOD seconds, as LIMIT/PERIOD: a question counts twice, its
# challenge and itself. A client is an IPv4 address or an IPv6 /64.
# CHAT_RATE_LIMIT=20/60
# The same, for the page's reads of the dataset: /api/meta, and
# /api/v/<snapshot>/<method>.
# QUERY_RATE_LIMIT=100/10
# The dataset the page and the chat's tools read, opened read-only: the
# directory `chatsbom snapshot build` publishes snapshots in, once
# `chatsbom warehouse build` has built the warehouse it reads (#132).
# Each question reads the snapshot its CURRENT names as the question
# starts, so one published later is served without a restart, and the
# page reads the one it was told of while CURRENT lists it (#144). It
# may name one snapshot's file instead. Unset, there is none: the page
# has nothing to read, and the chat cannot be on. A D1 export applied
# with sqlite3 is not a snapshot: the service does not start with one.
# WEB_SNAPSHOT=data/snapshots

# The chat, on DeepSeek (#140). Unset, the chat is off, and says so to
# anyone who asks; set, it needs WEB_SNAPSHOT too. Every question and
# every tool result it reads goes to DeepSeek.
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
# Where DeepSeek's OpenAI-format API is. A base URL decides where the
# key goes: change it only for a stand-in of your own.
# DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com
# The model: DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, by the name its pricing page gave it
# on 2026-09-29.
# CHAT_MODEL=deepseek-flash
# The most questions answered at once, whoever asks them. Each holds a
# model call and its share of the day's cap while it runs.
# CHAT_MAX_IN_FLIGHT=3
# The model's prices, US dollars per million tokens, as DeepSeek's
# pricing page said them on 2026-09-29: the spend cap holds each turn's
# worst case at them, and settles it at what it cost. In peak hours, a
# prompt token the context cache missed,
# CHAT_INPUT_USD_PER_MTOK=0.30
# one the cache served,
# CHAT_CACHED_INPUT_USD_PER_MTOK=0.006
# and a generated token, reasoning included.
# CHAT_OUTPUT_USD_PER_MTOK=1.20
# The same three off peak: half, at every other hour.
# CHAT_OFF_PEAK_INPUT_USD_PER_MTOK=0.15
# Cached, off peak.
# CHAT_OFF_PEAK_CACHED_INPUT_USD_PER_MTOK=0.003
# Generated, off peak.
# CHAT_OFF_PEAK_OUTPUT_USD_PER_MTOK=0.60
# The peak hours, in UTC, Monday to Friday, comma-separated. A turn
# that touches them is settled at peak, and so is one on a Chinese
# public holiday, which DeepSeek prices off peak.
# CHAT_PEAK_HOURS=01:00-04:00,06:00-10:00
